<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Æthelred the Unready</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 966–<a href="/years/1016">1016</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Ethelred the Unready, Æthelred II</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Æthelred the Unready was king of the English from 978 to 1016, apart from a brief exile in 1013–1014; his nickname means 'poorly advised'. His reign saw renewed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> attacks, which he tried to buy off with heavy payments of tribute, and the massacre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a> on St Brice's Day in 1002. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweyn-forkbeard">Sweyn Forkbeard</a> drove him into exile in Normandy in 1013; he returned after Sweyn's death but died as Cnut invaded.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/aethelred-the-unready.jpg" alt="Æthelred the Unready" width="320" height="540" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: See description · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ethelred_the_Unready.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Æthelred was the younger son of King Edgar and Queen Ælfthryth. Little is known of his early life; he was born during his father's reign. He came to the throne in 978, after his older half-brother Edward the Martyr was killed on arriving to visit Ælfthryth and Æthelred at Corfe in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorset">Dorset</a> on 18 March. No perpetrator is named in sources from before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a>. Later chroniclers and some historians blame Ælfthryth, while Ann Williams suggests an accidental affray. Æthelred was at most twelve and too young to be suspected, but he began in a weak position and was crowned only after more than a year. His nickname Unræd, 'ill counsel', puns on his name and is first recorded in the early thirteenth century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The country was governed by his father's advisers, including his mother, until he came of age in the mid-980s. He then took new advisers and granted them church property, but by the early 990s regretted this and saw <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> raids as God's punishment. Raids that were minor in the 980s became large attacks from the 990s. As the English were rarely victorious, the king and his advisers paid the Vikings tribute. The 990s and early 1000s were the most successful period of his reign, with able advisers and a monastic revival. In 1002 he ordered the St Brice's Day massacre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>, which historians see as a sign of increasing paranoia. By 1009 Eadric Streona had become his most powerful adviser.</p><p>In December 1013 Swein Forkbeard of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> and Æthelred fled to Normandy. When Swein died in February 1014 he returned and drove out Swein's son Cnut. In early 1015 civil war began when Eadric murdered close allies of Æthelred's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edmund-ironside">Edmund Ironside</a>. Cnut came back soon afterwards, and suspicion between father and son hampered their resistance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Æthelred died on 23 April 1016 and was buried in Old St Paul's Cathedral in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, where his tomb was destroyed in the Great Fire of 1666. Edmund was chosen king by the Londoners and Cnut at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southampton">Southampton</a>. Edmund was beaten at Assandun on 18 October, agreed to divide the kingdom with Cnut and died on 30 November, leaving Cnut king of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The main chronicle account of the reign was written after the Danish conquest and attributes the defeat to incompetence and cowardice, and historians since the 1970s distrust it as coloured by hindsight. Writers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norman-conquest">Norman Conquest</a> to the late twentieth century judged him a bad king, until a reassessment stressed the achievements of the 990s and early 1000s.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Æthelred the Unready” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Viking kings, the last of them Eric Bloodaxe, ruled York at intervals until 954, and under <b>Æthelred the Unready</b> renewed Danish attacks led to the conquest of England by Sweyn Forkbeard in 1013 and by his son Cnut in 1016.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Edmund Ironside, son of <b>Æthelred the Unready</b>, became king of the English and spent his seven-month reign fighting a Danish invasion led by Cnut the Great.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-23">23 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Edward the Confessor, son of <b>Æthelred the Unready</b> and Emma of Normandy, was crowned king of the English in Winchester Cathedral, having succeeded his half-brother Harthacnut in 1042.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-3">3 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Edward the Confessor, son of <b>Æthelred the Unready</b> and Emma of Normandy, succeeded his half-brother Harthacnut as king of the English.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-8">8 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>King <b>Æthelred the Unready</b> ordered the killing of Danish men in England.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-13">13 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Cnut ruled mainly from England, often from Winchester, keeping English institutions, issuing law codes and marrying Emma of Normandy, widow of <b>Æthelred the Unready</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/north-sea-empire">North Sea Empire</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">Kingdom of Wessex</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=998" aria-label="London on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the place of death of Æthelred the Unready"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4993 5724h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4993 5724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=51.51,-0.13&amp;year=998">What was here in 998 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
